            <div xml:id="d592">

                <pb facs="0669" n="659" sameAs="frus:frus1943China/pg659"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">811.20 Defense (M)/14316: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Atcheson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Chungking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1943-08-28T15:00:00+07:00">August 28, 1943—3
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 8 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1585. To Rosenthal, OEW, from Fowler: Department’s 1178, August 23. Smuggling
                    situation being currently investigated by us. Morris now quietly conducting
                    investigation. I will forward complete details on both antimony and tungsten as
                    soon as he reports. Although Chinese insist smuggled tungsten does not exceed 2
                    to 300 tons a year, that much larger amounts are handled is indicated by other
                    sources of information. [Fowler.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Atcheson</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
